+# Native Arena damage STARK research
+
+This is an unregistered local research adapter for one fixed public damage/parry
+transition. It invokes the actual execution backend in
+`stark/proof_managed_note_stark.rs`, also used by `RaceAdapterV1`. It neither
+registers an execution profile nor qualifies a native game relation. It cannot
+prove that a target was hit, that an attack was legal, that a multiplayer kill
+occurred, or that a chain settlement is valid.
+
+## Relation and binding
+
+The adapter reuses all equations in `arena_damage_air.rs`: 575 witness columns and
+663 constraints. It adds 17 equations binding the input and output columns to
+verifier-fixed public columns. The public statement is the ordered signed-i64
+vector documented by that AIR: health, stamina, raw damage, guarding, guard ticks,
+attacker and victim slots, facing XY, attacker XY, victim XY, next health,
+next stamina, damage dealt, and lethal flag. All fields and the exact AIR/adapter
+source identity are committed into the native transcript. The verifier evaluates
+the actual polynomial constraints; it does not accept host replay as proof.
+
+The experiment uses a late guard: incoming damage 85 is rounded up and halved to 43,
+stamina 35 becomes 25, health 120 becomes 77, and lethal is false. Every native trace
+row repeats this one relation. Repetition satisfies the backend's retained trace
+and masking geometry; it does not represent 8,192 different attacks or game ticks.
+The relation still requires a parent proof of the canonical facing direction,
+positions, weapon damage, attack order, selected living victim and legitimate hit,
+prior state, later death and bond allocation, and complete admitted match history.
+
+The existing nearest-target AIR in `arena_hit_air.rs` is not composed into this
+proof. Its equation tests and parent binding obligations remain separate.
+
+## Native security geometry and resources
+
+The adapter preserves the native execution suite: Goldilocks base field and
+quartic extension, six-lane Goldilocks Poseidon-x7 commitments/transcript,
+DEEP-ALI, 136 unique queries, 8x LDE, degree-four constraints and four composition
+chunks, 1,024-value terminal FRI vector with degree bound 143, native masking
+geometry, and 20-bit grinding. Proving obtains real operating-system entropy via
+the backend. It does not use a fixture seed, mock verifier or reduced query count.
+The backend remains independently unqualified; these parameters alone are not a
+release security review.
+
+The native trace has 8,192 rows, 583 base columns, 118 auxiliary columns, 60 fixed
+columns and 680 profile constraints (plus the native shared constraints). The
+backend computes a maximum canonical proof wire of 2,421,632 bytes, below the
+4 MiB research cap. Base and auxiliary LDE field matrices alone require about
+351 MiB; temporary matrices, commitments, masks and prover scratch add to that.
+Measured process memory and timings are recorded by the executable experiment,
+not inferred from this lower-bound allocation estimate.
+
+## Measured successful run
+
+On 2026-09-22, the native source harness completed with Rust 1.93.1, four Rayon
+threads, `rustc -C opt-level=3`, and the actual prebuilt workspace crypto rlibs
+(`fastpq_isi` uses the workspace development override `opt-level=2`). The machine
+was macOS on Apple Silicon with 128 GiB physical memory. Other workspace work was
+active; this is one local diagnostic, not an isolated benchmark or throughput
+qualification. Masking used real operating-system entropy, so nonce-search time
+is variable between runs.
+
+| Measurement | Observed value |
+| --- | ---: |
+| Actual proof bytes | 2,282,816 |
+| Native prove call, including mandatory self-verification | 522,431 ms |
+| Separate successful verifier call | 19,193 ms |
+| Whole executable, including seven negative verifier checks | 567.66 s |
+| Maximum resident set size | 584,663,040 bytes (about 558 MiB) |
+| macOS peak memory footprint | 547,521,328 bytes |
+| Swaps | 0 |
+| Negative verifications rejected | 7 of 7 |
+
+Proof SHA256:
+`f7cd23ba5598f774c7699a543f9910622eb2de78c84dd91b62766f0011edc82c`.
+The altered damage, next health, next stamina and lethal statements failed native
+transcript verification. Truncated and trailing-byte proofs failed canonical wire
+verification, and an altered proof failed its native trace-opening check. The
+independent 25 native equation tests also passed after the visibility-only
+refactor; no damage/parry arithmetic changed.
+
+The run's source manifest records adapter SHA256
+`866de4ea8e05199d575aecb32789c8e1c9825cd7cddcab797d789713a8ad6ac4`,
+native driver SHA256
+`8219e01c886487f45a7647e0d00c34ef40d25d14a12e741399331d737afc207d`,
+and verbatim base/extension-field extraction SHA256
+`3a8ba5e2e8a1c9208cfa8d40d29c69394bd76620cf35989404862e3a0a0145c5`.
+The complete manifest includes each AIR/backend source and linked dependency
+paths. It is a diagnostic provenance record, not a signed release attestation.
+That first run predates the harness hardening below: dependencies were selected
+by cache timestamp, and their byte hashes/full source-state seals were not
+captured before and after compilation. Its observed native proof/verification
+results stand, but exact Cargo/source attribution cannot be established
+retroactively. New build-only guard checks do not re-prove that earlier artifact.
+
+## Reproduction and artifacts
+
+Run from the isolated Iroha checkout after its native workspace dependencies have
+been built:
+
+```sh
+python3 scripts/check_arena_damage_stark.py --dependency-manifest /absolute/path/rlibs.json --threads 4
+python3 scripts/check_arena_damage_air.py
+python3 scripts/check_arena_damage_stark_test.py
+```
+
+The first script builds an optimized standalone Rust source harness. It compiles
+the unchanged native execution STARK modules directly and links the actual
+workspace `fastpq_isi`, `rand`, `rayon`, `sha2`, `iroha_data_model`, `thiserror` and
+`zeroize` rlibs. Its Goldilocks base and extension arithmetic is extracted verbatim
+from the actual native source, with checked unique source boundaries. It does not
+compile the unrelated `iroha_core` unit-test fixtures, alter Cargo manifests or
+module registration, or download replacement cryptography. This focused source
+harness is not a successful build/test of the entire node.
+
+Each new invocation creates an exclusive directory under
+`target/arena-damage-stark-research/runs/`, with distinct `build-` and `prove-`
+prefixes. `--output-dir` may supply another nonexistent directory; an existing
+directory is always rejected. Earlier successful and failed runs are preserved.
+The measured first-run files above remain in the original parent directory.
+
+Python 3, Node.js, Git and the workspace Rust toolchain are required. When more
+than one rlib exists for a dependency, the harness rejects ambiguity. The caller
+must explicitly select all seven artifacts in a JSON manifest with schema
+`iroha.arena.research_dependencies.v1` and a `dependencies` object keyed by
+`zeroize`, `thiserror`, `fastpq_isi`, `rayon`, `rand`, `sha2`, and
+`iroha_data_model`. Each value is exactly
+`{"path":"/absolute/target/debug/deps/libNAME-HASH.rlib","sha256":"64 lowercase hex digits"}`.
+Artifacts must reside in the selected target's dependency directory. The harness
+checks these pins against the actual bytes; it never chooses by modification
+time. With one unique artifact per library, it records their exact paths/hashes
+automatically. A pin identifies bytes, not their original Cargo/source build.
+
+Before compilation, after compilation, and after a proving run, the harness
+checks the repository's existing full tracked/untracked Git source-state owner,
+selected rlib hashes, verbose compiler identity, and hashes of the actual rustc,
+compiler driver and host standard-library artifacts. Any change, missing seal,
+compile error or failed proof check produces `run-status.json` with status
+`invalid` and `proof_verified:false`. Successfully compiled build-only runs are
+explicitly `build_complete`, also with `proof_verified:false`; they never invoke
+the prover. These guards establish stability of the observed inputs across a
+run, not a signed or hermetic Cargo build attestation.
+
+Each run contains:
+
+- `source-provenance.json`: exact source SHA256s, field extraction digest, linked
+  dependency paths/hashes, compiler version and Rayon thread count.
+- `seal-before.json`, `seal-after-compile.json`, `seal-after.json`: source-state,
+  dependency and actual compiler/standard-library byte identities.
+- `run-status.json`: final validity, explicit build/proof distinction, and hashes
+  of every produced run artifact. An invalid run must not be cited as successful
+  even if an earlier native step emitted proof or measurement bytes.
+- `damage-transition.stark`: actual native proof bytes.
+- `public-statement.txt`: exact public input/output vector.
+- `measurements.txt`: proof length/hash, separately measured prove/verify time,
+  and number of rejected negative verifications; written only on full success.
+- `run.log`: progress, results and macOS `/usr/bin/time -l` process resource
+  measurements, including maximum resident set size in bytes.
+
+The executable requires the valid proof to verify, then requires rejection under
+changed public damage, next health, next stamina and lethal output. It also
+requires rejection of truncated, trailing-byte and altered proof bytes. These
+checks test public binding and strict cryptographic wire verification; the 25
+separate equation tests exercise arithmetic adversaries and reference cases.
+No claimed benchmark extrapolates this single public relation to an entire FPS.
+
+Six filesystem/provenance guard tests cover exclusive directories, preserving
+prior evidence, ambiguous dependency rejection, exact hash pins, every seal
+component, missing seals, invalidating emitted evidence on seal drift, and
+build-only/failed-compile status. They do not substitute for a cryptographic run.
+
+The backend masks base and auxiliary trace coset values independently with
+976 fresh field coefficients per column. This is concrete blinding machinery;
+the experiment's 17 values are nevertheless all public and its intermediates
+are derivable. No useful confidentiality or independent zero-knowledge security
+claim is established here. Native masking, FRI and Fiat–Shamir privacy analysis
+remain separate qualification work.
