+# Native equipment catalogs, first release
+
+The native custody adapter accepts an equipment requirement only when an immutable
+catalog approves the exact native NFT identity and complete metadata hash. It
+never derives gameplay permissions from an NFT's owner-written JSON fields.
+
+## Trust and publication
+
+`RegisterGameEquipmentPublisherV1` installs exact publisher, application seed,
+collection, execution profile and role terms **only in signed genesis**. Its native
+handler rejects any later block, including calls through a custom executor or
+trigger. There is no update, deletion, self-registration or ownership fallback.
+No publisher account is installed by this change.
+
+Scopes pin exact profile and role identities. A later balance/protocol revision
+with a different compiled profile needs an explicitly designed authorization
+lifecycle or a genesis scope already naming that exact profile. The current
+first-release interface cannot expand publisher trust after genesis.
+
+`PublishGameEquipmentCatalogV1` requires that exact retained genesis scope, the
+authenticated publisher authority, actual network identity, current ownership of
+the collection and every member, and absence of active NFT reservations. Members
+are a nonempty, strictly ordered set of exact NFT identities with hashes of their
+complete canonical Norito metadata. Both trust and catalog records have
+domain-separated content identities. Duplicate publication is rejected.
+
+An application's identity is derived from network, trusted publisher and
+application seed. A later collection transfer cannot replace the retained
+publisher. This is exact identity/metadata approval, **not historical mint
+provenance**: the native NFT model has no immutable mint-instance field. Deletion,
+re-creation or metadata changes do not create another approved identity or hash.
+
+## Native admission and custody
+
+The compiled adapter selects a mandatory `GameResourceRequirementV1.catalog_id`
+from its immutable manifest parameters. Core resolves the retained catalog and
+checks its publication height, network, derived application, compiled profile,
+role, return policy, exact NFT identity and metadata. Wallet clauses must match
+the compiled requirements and the wallet signs the exact session manifest hash.
+A participant's supplied catalog identifier alone is never authoritative.
+
+After catalog validation, ordinary shared NFT custody checks the current owner,
+complete metadata hash, unique inverse reservation, non-signable custody identity
+and cumulative account/domain references. Preparation is read-only; applying the
+admission reserves assets atomically with participant funding. A listed NFT cannot
+be equipped. An equipped NFT cannot be listed, transferred, edited, deleted,
+equipped again or released through a sale/wager purpose. Terminal resource returns
+use the existing original-owner-only game payout path. Closed sale records cannot
+deliver later equipment reservations.
+
+## Retained state, queries and bounds
+
+`World.game_equipment_publishers` and `World.game_equipment_catalogs` are required
+canonical snapshot maps. Restoration validates content-derived keys, trusted
+scope, catalog network and publication height. Retained game resources are checked
+against catalog publication at their original admission height. Historical NFTs
+need not still be held by the publisher or even exist after all reservations end.
+State restoration checks the actual network and committed height for every
+catalog, including catalogs not referenced by a current session. The adjacent
+game, proof and NFT custody/sale maps are required too; missing first-release
+authoritative state is never reconstructed as empty history.
+
+`FindGameEquipmentPublisherById` and `FindGameEquipmentCatalogById` expose exact
+retained terms through ordinary native singular queries and their existing query
+authorization and bounded-result accounting.
+
+Each catalog permits at most 4096 members and 1 MiB of canonical descriptor bytes.
+NFT metadata hashing is limited to 64 KiB per member and 1 MiB total per
+publication. Participant admission retains its existing four-resource maximum.
+Publication gas charges descriptor bytes, members and bounded retained metadata
+work; admission reserves bounded catalog and metadata work per resource.
+
+## Validation and integration status
+
+- Production `cargo check -p connect_norito_bridge --lib` passed with native
+  instruction dispatch, executor classification, state persistence and queries.
+- Six tests compile the exact production pure policy source and native model,
+  instruction and query codecs. They reject untrusted ownership/self-declared
+  metadata, wrong authority/network/height, reserved items, metadata substitution,
+  catalog replay, wrong application/profile/role and oversized metadata. All pass.
+- The native resource exporter regenerated 16 mandatory first-release wire vectors.
+  `GameResourceRequirementV1` now requires `catalog_id`; the retired layout is not
+  decoded. Full JavaScript/native host verification is tracked separately.
+- `tests/native_game_equipment.rs` exercises the native instruction/query path,
+  genesis restriction, transactional trust rollback and publication metering.
+  `game_resources.rs` contains the private-custody exclusion regression.
+
+No live publisher trust, catalog, marketplace qualification or funded FPS profile
+is enabled. Existing stock adapters request no equipment. A complete source-bound
+FPS adapter must derive catalog requirements and constrain gameplay entitlements
+inside its full execution relation before any value-bearing Arena admission.
+The bounded AIR experiments do not satisfy that obligation.
